1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,026
A cinema ticket says row F, seat twelve.

2
00:00:03,026 --> 00:00:06,257
A train ticket says coach
S three, berth twenty one.

3
00:00:06,257 --> 00:00:09,124
A letter needs a house,
a street and a city.

4
00:00:09,524 --> 00:00:11,138
Each one is an address.

5
00:00:11,138 --> 00:00:14,119
A few numbers, and you
find exactly one spot.

6
00:00:14,569 --> 00:00:17,202
Diya is filling a form at her study table.

7
00:00:17,202 --> 00:00:19,343
Name, house, street, pin code.

8
00:00:19,693 --> 00:00:20,978
Then she wonders.

9
00:00:20,978 --> 00:00:23,509
An atom can hold dozens of electrons.

10
00:00:23,509 --> 00:00:26,410
How do chemists point to just one of them?

11
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:31,186
By the end of this video, you will
write the full address of any electron,

12
00:00:31,186 --> 00:00:32,632
with just four numbers.

13
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:35,227
First, where do electrons live?

14
00:00:35,577 --> 00:00:38,215
In the last episode we met the orbital.

15
00:00:38,215 --> 00:00:42,733
An orbital is a region around the
nucleus where an electron is most likely

16
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:43,747
to be found.

17
00:00:44,147 --> 00:00:45,297
Picture a cloud.

18
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:48,709
Where the dots are thick,
the electron is often there.

19
00:00:48,709 --> 00:00:51,252
Each orbital holds at most two electrons.

20
00:00:51,652 --> 00:00:55,240
And every orbital has an
address, made of quantum numbers.

21
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,824
There are four of them.

22
00:00:56,824 --> 00:00:58,613
n, l, m l, and m s.

23
00:00:59,213 --> 00:01:02,063
Let's build the addresses
on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:02,063 --> 00:01:04,765
Start with n, the
principal quantum number.

25
00:01:05,115 --> 00:01:08,366
n is a whole number: one,
two, three, and so on.

26
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:09,628
It tells the shell.

27
00:01:09,628 --> 00:01:14,088
A bigger n means a bigger shell, farther
from the nucleus, with more energy.

28
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:16,154
Shells also have letters.

29
00:01:16,154 --> 00:01:20,177
n equals one is K, two is L,
three is M, and four is N.

30
00:01:20,577 --> 00:01:23,024
Next, l, the azimuthal quantum number.

31
00:01:23,024 --> 00:01:27,303
It splits a shell into subshells,
and it sets the shape of the orbital.

32
00:01:27,703 --> 00:01:31,331
For a given n, l runs from
zero up to n minus one.

33
00:01:31,331 --> 00:01:34,580
So shell three has l
equals zero, one and two.

34
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:36,499
Each l has a letter.

35
00:01:36,499 --> 00:01:40,058
Zero is s, one is p, two
is d, and three is f.

36
00:01:40,458 --> 00:01:43,292
Third, m l, the magnetic quantum number.

37
00:01:43,292 --> 00:01:46,393
It tells which way the
orbital points in space.

38
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:49,298
m l runs from minus l to plus l.

39
00:01:49,298 --> 00:01:53,900
So an s subshell has one orbital,
p has three, and d has five.

40
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,954
Last, m s, the spin quantum number.

41
00:01:56,954 --> 00:02:01,602
The electron spins one of two ways,
plus one half or minus one half.

42
00:02:01,602 --> 00:02:04,113
We draw them as up and down arrows.

43
00:02:04,513 --> 00:02:09,320
So this electron in a three p orbital
has the address three, one, minus

44
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:10,915
one, plus one half.

45
00:02:10,915 --> 00:02:13,153
Its partner has minus one half.

46
00:02:13,803 --> 00:02:15,632
Now look at two addresses.

47
00:02:15,632 --> 00:02:17,828
n equals two with l equals one.

48
00:02:17,828 --> 00:02:20,281
And n equals two with l equals two.

49
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:21,814
Pause and think.

50
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:23,849
Which of these can really exist?

51
00:02:27,049 --> 00:02:28,181
Only the first.

52
00:02:28,181 --> 00:02:31,450
In shell two, l can be
zero or one, never two.

53
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,482
So there is no two d orbital.

54
00:02:34,132 --> 00:02:37,763
Now let's count the orbitals in
any shell, one step at a time.

55
00:02:38,113 --> 00:02:39,013
Step one.

56
00:02:38,996 --> 00:02:43,506
For a given n, l takes n values,
from zero to n minus one.

57
00:02:44,256 --> 00:02:45,162
Step two.

58
00:02:45,162 --> 00:02:50,075
Each l gives two l plus one
orbitals, one for each value of m l.

59
00:02:50,825 --> 00:02:51,727
Step three.

60
00:02:51,727 --> 00:02:52,736
Add them up.

61
00:02:52,736 --> 00:02:55,608
One, plus three, plus five, and so on.

62
00:02:55,608 --> 00:02:57,675
The total is always n squared.

63
00:02:58,425 --> 00:02:59,341
Step four.

64
00:02:59,341 --> 00:03:02,900
Each orbital holds two
electrons with opposite spins.

65
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:06,374
So a shell holds at most
two n squared electrons.

66
00:03:07,124 --> 00:03:08,244
Remember this.

67
00:03:08,244 --> 00:03:12,056
Four quantum numbers give
every electron its own address.

68
00:03:12,756 --> 00:03:14,491
Now, one worked example.

69
00:03:14,791 --> 00:03:18,420
How many orbitals are there in
the shell with n equals three?

70
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:20,605
And how many electrons can it hold?

71
00:03:21,105 --> 00:03:23,216
l can be zero, one or two.

72
00:03:23,216 --> 00:03:26,163
That gives three s, three p and three d.

73
00:03:26,913 --> 00:03:28,832
Three s has one orbital.

74
00:03:28,832 --> 00:03:30,357
Three p has three.

75
00:03:30,357 --> 00:03:31,826
Three d has five.

76
00:03:32,576 --> 00:03:34,982
One plus three plus five is nine.

77
00:03:34,982 --> 00:03:37,222
And three squared is also nine.

78
00:03:37,222 --> 00:03:38,124
It checks.

79
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:43,661
Two electrons in each orbital, so
the M shell holds eighteen electrons.

80
00:03:44,311 --> 00:03:45,774
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:46,124 --> 00:03:49,225
Many students let l go
all the way up to n.

82
00:03:49,225 --> 00:03:51,373
They write one p, or two d.

83
00:03:51,773 --> 00:03:53,842
But l stops at n minus one.

84
00:03:53,842 --> 00:03:55,480
Shell one has only s.

85
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:57,232
Shell two has s and p.

86
00:03:57,232 --> 00:04:00,181
The first d orbitals
appear in shell three.

87
00:04:00,831 --> 00:04:02,349
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:02,349 --> 00:04:07,178
In JEE Main and NEET, usually as which
set of quantum numbers is allowed,

89
00:04:07,178 --> 00:04:09,796
or how many orbitals or electrons fit.

90
00:04:10,396 --> 00:04:11,745
Exam level now.

91
00:04:11,745 --> 00:04:17,075
How many electrons in an atom can have
n equals four and m l equals minus

92
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:17,975
one?

93
00:04:17,658 --> 00:04:19,064
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:24,464 --> 00:04:26,012
The answer is B.

95
00:04:26,012 --> 00:04:27,296
Six electrons.

96
00:04:27,796 --> 00:04:31,327
For n equals four, l can
be zero, one, two or three.

97
00:04:31,827 --> 00:04:35,550
m l equals minus one needs
l to be at least one.

98
00:04:35,550 --> 00:04:39,439
So one orbital each in
four p, four d and four f.

99
00:04:39,939 --> 00:04:43,084
Three orbitals, two
electrons each, makes six.

100
00:04:43,084 --> 00:04:46,548
Option A forgot that each
orbital holds two electrons.

101
00:04:47,148 --> 00:04:48,607
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:48,607 --> 00:04:50,108
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:53,308 --> 00:04:56,144
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:04:56,544 --> 00:04:57,444
One.

105
00:04:57,070 --> 00:05:00,051
What values can l take when n equals four?

106
00:05:00,851 --> 00:05:01,751
Two.

107
00:05:01,387 --> 00:05:03,671
Why is there no two d orbital?

108
00:05:04,471 --> 00:05:05,371
Three.

109
00:05:05,071 --> 00:05:07,756
How many electrons can the N shell hold?

110
00:05:08,656 --> 00:05:13,230
Next time: why is a p orbital shaped
like a dumbbell, with nothing at its

111
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:14,130
centre?

112
00:05:13,859 --> 00:05:16,966
We find out in shapes
of s, p and d orbitals.
